t for either party could shift it further.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Indiana has voted Republican in every presidential election since 2008's near-miss, and its nine-point Republican lean at the presidential level sets the starting point for this open-seat contest. No incumbent is on the ballot once the term-limited president leaves office, and neither party has settled on a nominee, so the forecast rests on the state's underlying partisanship rather than candidate-specific evidence.\n\nThe 2026 primaries reinforced the picture: Trump-backed candidates swept Indiana Senate and legislative races, and the state party remains firmly aligned with the president. On the Democratic side, former South Bend mayor Pete Buttigieg leads early national polling for 2028, and his Indiana roots could shave a point or two off the Republican margin if he wins the nomination. Even so, the state's fundamentals point to a Republican favorite, with the race rated Lean Republican until polling or expert ratings say otherwise.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1505, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2049, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2014, "margin": -9.0}]}